1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,087
Welcome to LEOTherm, a thermal simulation workbench for low Earth orbit satellite research.

2
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:08,609
Version one point two point one starts in English.

3
00:00:08,609 --> 00:00:12,914
This introduction uses the actual application, computed results and generated reports.

4
00:00:12,914 --> 00:00:19,567
We will start from an example, run a thermal simulation, explore optional stages, and finish with a

5
00:00:19,567 --> 00:00:21,915
report that records what was calculated.

6
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:27,772
The Quick start page provides ready to use templates, a task wizard and saved projects.

7
00:00:27,772 --> 00:00:30,209
For a first run, choose First simulation.

8
00:00:30,209 --> 00:00:34,038
The wizard asks for a task name, duration and output interval.

9
00:00:34,038 --> 00:00:38,216
You can also copy a readable template and change a few fields.

10
00:00:38,216 --> 00:00:42,045
The Windows executable needs free MATLAB Runtime twenty twenty one b.

11
00:00:42,045 --> 00:00:45,875
Source users start the application with launch underscore g u i.

12
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:51,993
On Simulation, review the start time, duration, orbit altitude, inclination and orbit direction.

13
00:00:51,993 --> 00:00:58,754
Select the satellite attitude, then enable direct sunlight, reflected sunlight and Earth infrared radiation as needed.

14
00:00:58,754 --> 00:01:00,444
Check the displayed units.

15
00:01:00,444 --> 00:01:03,824
Warmup orbits reduce the influence of starting temperatures.

16
00:01:03,824 --> 00:01:08,895
Periodic convergence checks whether repeated orbits have reached a stable thermal cycle.

17
00:01:08,895 --> 00:01:15,233
The example network is a reference model, so mission specific studies need their own parameters.

18
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:19,905
The thermal network represents satellite parts as connected temperature nodes.

19
00:01:19,905 --> 00:01:23,960
Set heat capacity, optical properties, internal power and thermal links.

20
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:30,448
The solver calculates heat entering the satellite, heat exchanged between parts and heat radiated to space.

21
00:01:30,448 --> 00:01:34,503
It then integrates the temperature of each part over time.

22
00:01:34,503 --> 00:01:40,180
Receiver, antenna and oscillator roles help identify the response channels used in derived analyses.

23
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:42,736
Check the task before running.

24
00:01:42,736 --> 00:01:46,220
Required inputs are checked for the stages you selected.

25
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:48,544
The basic nodal simulation always runs.

26
00:01:48,544 --> 00:01:53,964
In this example, the two hour task actually completed and passed its numerical checks.

27
00:01:53,964 --> 00:01:58,223
Temperature and illumination plots show the response through sunlight and shadow.

28
00:01:58,223 --> 00:02:02,869
The Results and export page provides the run status and report controls.

29
00:02:02,869 --> 00:02:09,064
Numerical acceptance means the declared checks passed; measured satellite data are needed to test physical agreement.

30
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:14,430
Enable Parameter sweep when you want to compare conditions within one task.

31
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:19,160
This demonstration runs six cases: two altitudes and three target beta angles.

32
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:23,890
Beta describes the angle between the Sun direction and the orbital plane.

33
00:02:23,890 --> 00:02:27,437
Another mode varies dates, inclinations and orbit directions directly.

34
00:02:27,437 --> 00:02:30,590
Check each case status before comparing temperature ranges.

35
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:36,109
These short demonstration runs include starting transients and do not establish a global optimum.

36
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:41,437
The geometry stage accepts a surface model and a tetrahedral volume mesh.

37
00:02:41,437 --> 00:02:44,952
Declare mesh units, material properties, contacts and thermal boundaries.

38
00:02:44,952 --> 00:02:50,811
This example actually runs surface to volume coupling: calculated surface heat loads drive internal conduction.

39
00:02:50,811 --> 00:02:55,107
The current coupling is one way and requires matching boundary geometry.

40
00:02:55,107 --> 00:03:01,747
The task report is the source for the newly computed results; the geometry panel may still display

41
00:03:01,747 --> 00:03:02,918
an older result.

42
00:03:02,918 --> 00:03:06,043
Inspect heat flow accounting as well as temperatures.

43
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:11,210
Engineering thermal model exchange provides a route for reusing existing models.

44
00:03:11,210 --> 00:03:18,257
Import a supported exchange file, inspect it, define the node mapping and preview the changes before applying

45
00:03:18,257 --> 00:03:18,672
them.

46
00:03:18,672 --> 00:03:22,817
Custom adapters and external solver interfaces are available to developers.

47
00:03:22,817 --> 00:03:26,134
A successful file import checks the declared format.

48
00:03:26,134 --> 00:03:30,694
Units, materials and boundaries still need to represent the intended device.

49
00:03:30,694 --> 00:03:35,253
This introduction shows the exchange interface, not a commercial solver execution.

50
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:40,699
Telemetry and validation maps time, measured temperatures and their corresponding model nodes.

51
00:03:40,699 --> 00:03:46,298
Check the time span, units, missing values and exact time alignment before comparing results.

52
00:03:46,298 --> 00:03:53,096
This demonstration runs the built in synthetic example, which teaches the workflow and is not an in

53
00:03:53,096 --> 00:03:53,896
orbit validation.

54
00:03:53,896 --> 00:03:58,295
For your satellite, use traceable measurements and independently held out days.

55
00:03:58,295 --> 00:04:01,894
The report includes the scored comparison and its limits.

56
00:04:01,894 --> 00:04:04,293
Missing data are not silently interpolated.

57
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:11,348
Device calibration uses nominal device parameters, explicit allowed ranges and penalties for moving away from the reference

58
00:04:11,348 --> 00:04:11,727
values.

59
00:04:11,727 --> 00:04:15,140
Split whole days into training, validation and test sets.

60
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:18,554
Only accepted fitted parameters should drive a later simulation.

61
00:04:18,554 --> 00:04:23,864
This screen explains the configuration; no new calibration fit is claimed in this introduction.

62
00:04:23,864 --> 00:04:30,311
Use separate test data to judge agreement, and review the warnings before treating a fit as a

63
00:04:30,311 --> 00:04:31,449
validated device model.

64
00:04:32,050 --> 00:04:35,393
The final analysis report is the main deliverable.

65
00:04:35,393 --> 00:04:40,827
It records the task settings, temperature statistics, calculated response metrics and numerical acceptance.

66
00:04:40,827 --> 00:04:45,424
Each completed optional stage adds its own findings, charts and tables.

67
00:04:45,424 --> 00:04:48,767
Skipped stages are identified in the stage record.

68
00:04:48,767 --> 00:04:53,365
Interpret the findings together with the model assumptions and warmup checks.

69
00:04:53,365 --> 00:04:59,634
The reports shown here were generated by the actual base, sweep, geometry and telemetry tasks.

70
00:05:00,250 --> 00:05:05,522
Each run also retains the input snapshot, frozen model, computed data and stage status.

71
00:05:05,522 --> 00:05:11,925
Save the project to continue your work, then run changed settings in a new directory so earlier

72
00:05:11,925 --> 00:05:13,055
evidence remains available.

73
00:05:13,055 --> 00:05:19,457
Start with the simple template, add only the stages you need, and finish by reading the report.

74
00:05:19,457 --> 00:05:21,717
The language selector still supports Chinese.

75
00:05:21,717 --> 00:05:24,729
Your saved project remembers its language and configuration.
